{
  "rules": [
    "Correct for severe underestimation of upward momentum: When history shows a sharp price spike (e.g., >3 EUR in 2 days) and sentiment is neutral/low, the model fails to capture the breakout, consistently forecasting flat or slightly declining paths while true prices surge 10-15 EUR higher. Increase forecast slope by 0.5-1.0 EUR/day for the first 10 days following a breakout.",
    "Apply aggressive mean reversion after peaks: The model exhibits a 'lag' in recognizing trend exhaustion, continuing to forecast gradual increases even after the true price peaks and begins a sharp decline (e.g., dropping from ~89 to ~65). Reduce forecasts by 2-3 EUR immediately after the 10-day horizon if the 18-day history shows a peak followed by a drop.",
    "Ignore sentiment signals during high-volatility regimes: Positive sentiment scores (0.20-0.33) in the history do not correlate with the model's ability to predict the magnitude of the subsequent rally. Do not rely on sentiment to adjust the baseline; instead, use the momentum of the last 3 days of price history to override the model's flat projection.",
    "Adjust for terminal value overshoot: The model consistently overestimates prices in the final 5-7 days of the 30-day horizon during downtrends, failing to account for the speed of the collapse. Apply a downward correction of 1.5-2.0 EUR to the final week's forecast when the 18-day history ends below the 30-day average.",
    "Detect 'flat-line' bias: The model assumes a mean-reverting equilibrium around the current price (e.g., ~72-79) regardless of the trend direction. If the 18-day history has a clear trend (up or down), shift the entire 30-day forecast curve parallel to the trend direction rather than anchoring to the last observed price."
  ]
}
